<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ho Chi Minh City</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saigon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> City, long known as Saigon, is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, on the Saigon River near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> settlement taken over by Vietnamese in the 17th century, it was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1859 and became the capital of French Cochinchina and later of South Vietnam. After its fall to North Vietnamese forces on 30 April 1975 it was renamed in 1976 after Ho Chi Minh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh City" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steffen Schmitz (more photos) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ho_Chi_Minh_City,_City_Hall,_2020-01_CN-03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Paul Doumer, governor-general from 1897 to 1902, gave the federation its lasting shape: he created a general budget and federal services, subordinated the Vietnamese court, and moved the seat of government from <b>Saigon</b> to Hanoi in 1902.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From his capital at Gia Dinh (<b>Saigon</b>), taken in 1788, Nguyen Anh built up a strong army and navy, and after Nguyen Hue’s death in 1792 the Tay Son weakened.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 30 April 1975 North Vietnamese tanks entered <b>Saigon</b> and the government of the Republic of Vietnam surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle exposed weaknesses in the <b>Saigon</b> army and US optimism about the war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic, based in <b>Saigon</b>, was dominated by French interests and a small southern Francophile elite.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-republic-of-cochinchina">Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Paris Peace Accords of 1973 and the fall of <b>Saigon</b> in April 1975, the DRV merged with the Republic of South Vietnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam</a></li></ul></section>
